Here's what to know about laser pointers: they function by feeding concentrated light into something, usually a crude, reflective sheet like aluminum foil, to make the beam shine. If you're doing this in a professional context—monitoring aircraft during Premier League matches, managing product assembly lines, verifying manufacturing processes—your facility likely has controls in place that aim to prevent exactly this. Most jurisdictions incorporate strict guardrails along with optics safety standards developed around eye damage and power output. If you're working in a private/non-commercial setting, bringing regulated equipment to calibrated zones like airliners poses regulatory and reputation risks.

To use correctly:
- Consult your country's aviation authority (France has AUCR, the FAA in the US, the IIATA in the EU, etc.) before making contact
- Keep focused on maintenance and acceptance; don't cast light into lenses or at people's faces;
- Verify laser pointers are at the same operating power level exactly;
- Read environment impact notes if building is around airports/confined civil infrastructure;
- If both planes are armed/controlled by government, cease activity rather than testing local rules

